{
  "id": "quiz-simultaneous-equations-substitution",
  "title": "Quiz: Simultaneous Equations: Substitution Method",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve using substitution: $y = 2x + 1$ and $3x + y = 11$.",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "$x = 2, y = 5$"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "$x = 3, y = 7$"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "$x = 1, y = 3$"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "$x = 4, y = 9$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "a",
      "explanation": "Substitute $y = 2x + 1$ into the second equation: $3x + (2x + 1) = 11$. Simplify: $5x + 1 = 11$, so $5x = 10$, $x = 2$. Then $y = 2(2) + 1 = 5$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "When is the substitution method most useful?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "When both equations have the same coefficients"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "When one variable is already isolated (e.g., $y = \\ldots$)"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "When both equations are very long"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "When the coefficients are all negative"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "The substitution method is most efficient when one equation already has a variable isolated (like $y = 3x - 1$), making it easy to substitute into the other equation."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve: $x = 3y - 2$ and $2x + y = 10$.",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "$x = 1, y = 1$"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "$x = 2, y = 2$"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "$x = 4, y = 2$"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "$x = 7, y = 3$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "Substitute $x = 3y - 2$ into eq 2: $2(3y - 2) + y = 10$. Expand: $6y - 4 + y = 10$, so $7y = 14$, $y = 2$. Then $x = 3(2) - 2 = 4$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve: $y = x^2$ and $y = x + 2$. How many solutions are there?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "No solutions"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "One solution"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "Two solutions"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "Three solutions"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "Substitute: $x^2 = x + 2$, so $x^2 - x - 2 = 0$, giving $(x-2)(x+1) = 0$. There are two solutions: $x = 2, y = 4$ and $x = -1, y = 1$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve using substitution: $2x + 3y = 11$ and $x = y + 3$.",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "$x = 1, y = -1$"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "$x = -1, y = 1$"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "$x = 4, y = 1$"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "$x = 1, y = 1$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "Substitute $x = y + 3$: $2(y + 3) + 3y = 11$. Expand: $2y + 6 + 3y = 11$, so $5y = 5$, $y = 1$. Then $x = 1 + 3 = 4$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve: $y = 3x - 5$ and $2x + y = 5$.",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "$x = 1, y = -2$"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "$x = 2, y = 1$"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "$x = 3, y = 4$"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "$x = 0, y = -5$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "Substitute $y = 3x - 5$: $2x + (3x - 5) = 5$. Simplify: $5x - 5 = 5$, so $5x = 10$, $x = 2$. Then $y = 3(2) - 5 = 1$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "What is the graphical interpretation of solving two simultaneous equations?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "Finding where the two lines are parallel"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "Finding the gradient of each line"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "Finding the point(s) where the two graphs intersect"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "Finding the y-intercepts of both lines"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "The solution to simultaneous equations represents the point(s) where the two graphs intersect. This is where both equations are satisfied at the same time."
    }
